A||N||11||DaveL||Front Compartment Light||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||10-05-2000||02:49 PM||||On an 87 SE, where is the front compartment light? Mine has never worked. Got to looking for it and couldn't find it.<BR>What the ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||10-05-2000||02:58 PM||||It should be in the center of the firewall next to the A/C dryer facing down.  It only comes on when the hood is opened and the parking or headlights are on.  You will see the switch on the top in the center, it looks like the door light switch. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||10-05-2000||03:01 PM||||Don't feel bad about not finding it..I've had my Fiero 13 years and was told about the under the hood light two weeks ago.  Open the hood, turn on the parking lights and see if some light comes from a housing on the passenger side of the trunk next to the a/c collector.  I was lucky, my light still worked after 13 years.  I didn't know about it or that you have to have the parking light switch on for it to work.  Not too useful unless you want to access the jack to change a flat tire at night.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaveL||10-05-2000||03:07 PM||||DANG! Boy, now I do feel stupid. Duh<BR>I've never had it open when the lights were on. The light may work after all. I'm going to look right now. Take care of my light work first....||||reg||10
Z||000004||rockcrawl||10-05-2000||05:18 PM||||Her's another funny fact about that light. It was an option, but all cars had the socket in the harness. If the car being built was to have the underhood light option, they screwed on a little metal bracket and stuck a bulb in, if not they just taped the socket back against the harness. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-05-2000||06:39 PM||||When I first saw the wiring for this in the various diagrams I thought it was a joke. I don't even want to know why anyone at GM thougth tieing the light to the parking lights was a good idea.||||reg||1
Z||000006||sombre||10-05-2000||08:56 PM||||Here's annother question along the same lines.<P> Anybody know where the rear trunk light switch is on an '86 SE....<BR>  My 84 had a pin switch in an "L" bracket on the left rear of engine compartment.My 86 does not nor does it look like the bracket was ever there.The bulb was out when I got the car butt the way it's wired now it's on all the time so I just took out the bulb again till I find out where it's supposed to be.<BR>                           Thanks<BR>                                  Greg||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||10-05-2000||09:26 PM||||Here's another little known fact......late model '88s did not have the underhood light and there was no provision for one in the wiring harness.  It was speculated that GM had some plan for the Fiero in later production years, but they killed it before anything happened...who knows why they went through the hassles of reworking that harness?<P>My low mileage '88 GT (VIN # 07940) has the light, but my daily driver '88 GT (VIN# 21324) doesn't.<P>Kind of interesting.||||reg||10
Z||000008||fieroparts.com||10-05-2000||10:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>Here's another little known fact......late model '88s did not have the underhood light and there was no provision for one in the wiring harness.  It was speculated that GM had some plan for the Fiero in later production years, but they killed it before anything happened...who knows why they went through the hassles of reworking that harness?<P>My low mileage '88 GT (VIN # 07940) has the light, but my daily driver '88 GT (VIN# 21324) doesn't.<P>Kind of interesting.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>this is true!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||286SE'S||10-06-2000||01:52 PM||||SOMBRE wrote:<P>"Anybody know where the rear trunk light switch is on an '86 SE...."<P>Its in the latch of the deck lid part of the electric release. look for a small plastic lever on the cutout that the loop <BR>in the trunk space below hooks into. Yours may be broken off, leaving the light on.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DaveL||10-06-2000||02:18 PM||||I think I figured out why GM hid the underdeck light. It was so no one would notice that the engine was missing. They just like keeping us in the dark. I get the image of some horn rim glassed nerd working late at night alone in the design division, several pencils behind each ear, pocket protector full of unused pens in shirt pockets, giggling insanely to himself, "Huh huh, just wait until someone tries to figure out why we did this...." Why oh why do I like this car so much!!??!!||||reg||6
Z||000011||StuGood||10-06-2000||06:12 PM||||My guess is, they figured as long as you're changing the tire, you might as well have at least the parking lights on, so nobody hits your car in the dark.<P>BTW, did any Fieros come equipped with an engine compartment light (not the trunk lamp, I mean one that shines onto the engine)?  I've never seen it.  Maybe GM <B>did</B> want to hide something!  ||||reg||6
